# .gitattributes -- line-ending contract for a project running the governance
# harness. Copy this block into the project's own .gitattributes at the repo
# root (append to an existing file; do not replace rules you already have).
#
# WHY A PROJECT NEEDS THIS AT ALL. The harness ships its shell scripts with LF
# and its own repo is clean. The corruption happens on YOUR side: with no
# .gitattributes, git falls back to each developer's core.autocrlf, which on
# Windows rewrites text files to CRLF on checkout. The installed scripts are
# then CRLF in your working tree, get committed that way, and Docker bakes them
# into the image. Nothing in the harness can prevent that from the outside --
# only a .gitattributes committed in THIS repo can.
#
# policy-ci checks the result, not the cause: `crlf:shell-scripts-are-lf` fails
# on any .sh whose first 400 bytes contain CRLF, and
# `bom:powershell-has-utf8-bom` fails on any .ps1 without a UTF-8 BOM.
#
# NOTE: `eol=` governs the WORKING TREE. Files already committed with the wrong
# endings keep them until they are renormalised:
#     git add --renormalize .   &&   git status
# Run that once after adding this file, or the rules look applied and nothing
# on disk has actually changed.

# Baseline: let git decide for anything not pinned below.
* text=auto

# --- LF, always. These are read by a Linux kernel or a Linux container. ------

# CRLF here ends the shebang with \r, so Linux looks for an interpreter
# literally named "/bin/sh\r" and exec fails with
#   "exec /usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory"
# The error names the INTERPRETER, not the script, and the file is present and
# executable the whole time -- which is why this costs hours, not minutes.
# One project crash-looped 115 times on this exact byte.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Dockerfile: a CRLF line ending is carried into ENV/ARG/LABEL values and into
# every RUN command, so a trailing \r ends up inside PATH or inside a filename.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# YAML: CRLF makes a trailing \r part of the scalar, so "value" becomes
# "value\r". Docker Compose commands, Kubernetes manifests and CI shell steps
# then run with an invisible \r in an argument.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Anything with a shebang but no extension: git cannot infer these, so list
# them explicitly in your own project, e.g.
#   entrypoint      text eol=lf
#   scripts/deploy  text eol=lf

# --- CRLF, always. These are read by Windows PowerShell / cmd.exe. -----------

# PowerShell and batch stay CRLF so they behave in the shell that runs them.
# NOTE on .ps1: eol=crlf does NOT give you a UTF-8 BOM, and Windows PowerShell
# 5.1 reads a BOM-less .ps1 in the system ANSI codepage. On a CP932 (Japanese)
# machine, bytes 0x81-0x9F and 0xE0-0xFC are LEAD bytes that consume the byte
# after them, so a line ending in a Vietnamese character or an em dash swallows
# its own newline -- and the NEXT LINE OF CODE disappears from the parsed
# script while it still exits 0. Save .ps1 as "UTF-8 with BOM"; git cannot do
# it for you, which is why policy-ci checks it separately.
#
# This rule also applies to the harness's own .ps1 under .harness/ and
# tools/harness-bundle/, which the bundle ships as LF and the install receipt
# hashes as LF. That is fine: doctor, harness-verify and the installer compare
# EOL-insensitively, with the BOM kept as well as stripped (since 1.8.11; before
# it a BOM'd .ps1 checked out as CRLF read as drift -- B-77). A project-side
# `.harness/**/*.ps1 text eol=lf` override is harmless but no longer needed.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
*.psd1          text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# --- Binary: never munge, never diff. ---------------------------------------
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.gz            binary
*.zip           binary
